speechtext:
Of course my hon. friend will have an opportunity a little later to disprove that if he desires to do so, but for the time being I maintain that that was the situation. Now, that brings us down to July, 1931, and to the decisions that were made at the end of the crop year 1930-31. We had an entirely new set-up; we were starting with a new set of conditions, and I am quite sure that the leader of the opposition will not contend that the dominion government was not responsible for Mr. McFarland being there from July, 1931, on. It is obvious to everyone that the first proposal was that a corporation was to be set up. This corporation was to be headed by Mr. McFarland, and on the condition of that corporation being set up, headed by Mr. McFarland, the federal government was prepared to take an active part in financing the crop of 1931. When that was not agreeable to Mr. McFarland another arrangement was made involving Mr. McFarland, involving the wheat pool, and involving the government of Canada, and I believe that all three were parties to the agreement which was reached.
At the time we were in the position that a certain situation existed with regard to the grain stocks of the different pools. I stated at the beginning and stated yesterday that there were really five pools in two of the provinces and only one pool in the other. Each of the three provinces had a wheat pool, but only two of the provinces, Saskatchewan and Manitoba, had what are called coarse grain pools. Each one of these provinces had a flax pool, a rye pool, a barley pool and an oat pool, and the persons who were delivering grain to one of these pools were not necessarily the same as those who were delivering grain to other pools. Those who sent grain to the barley pool did not necessarily send grain to the rye pool; those who sent to the oat pool did not necessarily send to the flax pool, and so on. They were separate pools and to a degree at least were composed of different persons.
During the crop year 1930-31, with the grain of which we are now concerned in so far as it was delivered to the pools, there was delivered to the oat pool 5,317.000 bushels; to the barley pool, 6,356.000 bushels; to the rye pool, 2,257,000 bushels, and to the flax pool, 1,431,000 bushels;
